          "source_quote": "πρόφασιν εὑροῦσα τὴν πρόνοιαν καὶ τεκούσης στοργήν",
          "english_quote": "by finding a pretext in providence and a mother’s affection",
          "issue": "“Providence” is lexically possible, but it commonly suggests divine governance in modern English. πρόνοια also means care, concern, or forethought; its pairing here with maternal affection supports that alternative. The draft does not disclose this materially different interpretation or explain its choice.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use “taking care and a mother’s affection as a pretext,” with a note explaining the contextual choice and preserving “providence” as an alternative. If retaining “providence,” document why divine providence is understood here and preserve the care/concern alternative without assigning an otherwise unstated possessor.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον ὑμεῖς κἀγὼ σὺν ὑμῖν τὴν σήμερον διαμείνωμεν",
          "english_quote": "Rather, let you and I with you endure today",
          "issue": "The English construction “let you and I” is ungrammatical, and the placement of “with you” makes the preserved repetition difficult to follow. The underlying meaning is nevertheless represented.",
          "suggested_revision": "Consider “Rather, let us—you, and I with you—hold out for today.” This retains both the shared exhortation and the source’s repeated reference to “you.”",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
