          "source_quote": "τὴν αἰσθανομένην ἢ γενομένου τινὸς ἐραστοῦ",
          "english_quote": "a woman who becomes aware, or who finds that someone has become a suitor",
          "issue": "The main rendering can be defended by carrying the perception expressed in αἰσθανομένην into the second alternative. However, γενομένου τινὸς ἐραστοῦ itself expresses someone’s becoming a suitor, not a separately stated act of finding out. The note identifies the genitive absolute but does not preserve the viable alternative in which it supplies a circumstance of flight. Its offered smoother alternative explicitly suppresses the printed disjunction and therefore does not adequately represent that alternative reading of this edition.",
          "suggested_revision": "Expand the note to state that “who finds” carries perception over by ellipsis rather than translating a second printed perception verb. Preserve the alternative attachment as “or when someone has become a suitor,” qualifying the circumstances of the required flight, and explain why the main text instead treats both alternatives as ways the danger becomes apparent. Retain the supplied ἢ; no source correction or additional verification is required merely because the construction is compressed.",
